  "answers": [
    "**Short answer: no.** Your local Google data for October 2, 2026 has only 3 heart rate readings, and they cover a short exercise window, not your whole day.\n\n**What's there:**\n- 10:00:00, 10:01:00, and 10:02:00 local time\n- Values: 100, 101, and 102 bpm\n- Average: 101 bpm, range 100\u2013102 bpm\n\n**The sampling limitation:**\n- Google's export for this day is labeled \"exercise windows only.\" The provider sent heart rate only during an activity period, so the readings describe that stretch of about three minutes.\n- Resting, sleeping, and other daytime heart rate are not in the data. Silence in those hours doesn't mean your heart rate was zero or unrecorded; it means it wasn't exported.\n- Three readings are too few to describe even the exercise window well. They can't show peak or lowest heart rate for the session.\n- A whole-day summary would need all-day heart rate data. Google offers this as a separate option (\"full heart rate,\" roughly 37 MB per day). I haven't pulled it because you didn't ask for a new import.\n\n**Other notes:**\n- These records are from an earlier local import. The Google API connection isn't currently verified, so I couldn't check whether newer data exists. Running a sync or re-import would require a live connection.\n- This is information about your data, not medical advice. If you have concerns about your heart rate, talk to a clinician."
